{
  "slideIndex": 1,
  "section": "HTTP Caching",
  "titleRegister": "label",
  "contents": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Cache-Control sets the storage and validation contract"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Freshness directives define reuse without contacting the origin: max-age applies generally, while s-maxage targets shared caches.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "no-store forbids storage, whereas no-cache permits storage but requires successful validation before reuse.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "ETag provides the validator: the client or CDN sends If-None-Match, and a matching representation elicits 304 Not Modified from the origin.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A 304 reuses the cached representation and updates applicable metadata; it carries no new response body.",
          "bullet": false
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Contrast freshness and storage rules on the left with ETag revalidation on the right. Emphasize that no-cache is not no-store, and that 304 reuses the cached body rather than returning a new one."
}

{
  "slideIndex": 2,
  "section": "HTTP Caching",
  "titleRegister": "label",
  "contents": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Stale content is an explicit exception, not a default CDN behavior"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "A fresh shared-cache response is served immediately; when stale, the CDN first attempts validator-based revalidation with If-None-Match.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "stale-while-revalidate permits serving stale content during asynchronous background validation.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "stale-if-error permits stale content during an origin failure, but only within its permitted stale window.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A client max-stale request can permit stale acceptance, while response directives such as must-revalidate constrain or forbid stale reuse.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Implementation rule: evaluate shared-cache freshness first, then revalidation, and only then an explicitly permitted stale path.",
          "bullet": false
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Use the decision path in order: fresh response, validator-based revalidation, stale-while-revalidate, then stale-if-error or max-stale only when applicable. s-maxage governs shared-cache freshness when present."
}
